    "body": "## Problem\n\n`roslyn-language-server` requires a transport flag even when only asking for the version:\n\n```console\n$ roslyn-language-server --version; echo $?\nExpected either --stdio or --pipe <name>.\n4\n\n$ roslyn-language-server --stdio --version\n5.12.0-1.26426.8+3aeb96c9ecc56a5ee483558f9e648e33e7bfe756\n```\n\nSo `supports_autoload()` always returned `false`, and every C# buffer showed:\n\n```\nroslyn_ls: minimum required version is 5.8.0; projects won't be loaded\n```\n\neven though `--autoLoadProjects` worked and projects were loaded.\n\nThe check also compared the parsed version against `''`, but `vim.version.parse()` returns `nil` on failure, so a zero-exit probe with unparseable output could error in `vim.version.ge()`.\n\n## Solution\n\n- Build the probe from the configured `cmd` (minus `--autoLoadProjects`) and append `--version`. This includes `--stdio` for the default config and also handles the documented `dotnet <dll>` launcher correctly.\n- Compare against `nil` instead of `''` when parsing the version.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Verified the probe against the Mason-installed `roslyn-language-server` 5.12.0-1.26426.8: no warning.\n- Verified with a fake server: old version (`5.7.0`) still warns, missing binary warns, and exit 0 with unparseable output no longer errors.\n- Verified the documented `dotnet <dll>` path probes the DLL, not the .NET SDK version.\n- Real end-to-end: `nvim --clean` + `vim.lsp.enable('roslyn_ls')` attached the client and loaded the test project with no version warning.\n- `stylua --check .`, luals check, codespell, `vusted ./test`, and `.github/ci/lint.sh` all pass.\n\nFixes #4562.\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-11-02T13:18:34Z",
